# The source tree IS the repository (v1.86.0).
#
# It used to be excluded. The rule below read:
#
#     # The source is delivered as yagura-v0.35.0-source.tar.gz + a patch,
#     # NOT as committed plaintext — committing the raw tree trips the
#     # project's own fake-secret test fixtures under GitHub push protection.
#     /yagura-v*/
#
# That excluded 191 of 428 files, and nobody had measured what it cost:
#
#   - go.mod was not tracked, so **a clone of this repository could not build**;
#   - 100 _test.go files were not tracked — the very tests this project claims
#     mechanically enforce its promises;
#   - 36 production .go files were not tracked;
#   - LICENSE, NOTICE, CONTRIBUTING.md, SECURITY.md were not tracked;
#   - .github/workflows/*.yml were not tracked, so GitHub had **zero registered
#     workflows**: CI had never run once, and release.yml — the SBOM / Sigstore /
#     SLSA-3 pipeline — could never fire. The published claim that publication was
#     blocked by a 403 on tag push was wrong: there was nothing to trigger.
#
# The stated cause was worth checking rather than inheriting — and checking it
# produced a mixed verdict worth recording.
#
#   - The claim was RIGHT that push protection blocks: the first push of v1.86.0
#     was rejected (GH013). It blocked on a **Slack Incoming Webhook URL** in two
#     secretscan fixtures, now written as split string concatenations so no
#     committed byte sequence matches while the tests keep their real shape.
#   - The claim was WRONG about the scale of the remedy. Exactly 2 files needed a
#     one-line change. Excluding 191 files — go.mod, the CI, the test suite —
#     to avoid editing 2 was never proportionate, and its cost was never measured.
#
# A caution for whoever revisits this: an ad-hoc grep for the token shapes you
# happen to think of is not an audit. Mine checked five patterns and missed the
# Slack webhook entirely. This project's own scanner (internal/secretscan) reports
# 36 high/critical findings across 8 files here, while GitHub's push protection
# blocked on exactly one pattern — the two tools disagree by a wide margin, so
# neither one's silence is evidence.
#
# Build output only, from here on.
/yagura-v*/bin/
/.claude/
